{
  "arm": "claudecli_opus5_mcp_plumbline_rejection_selection_v7",
  "contract": "Short-lived state - an in-flight fetch's status, a pending selection, an interaction or animation still in progress - must record the scope it was started in and be torn down when that scope changes, because the object that owns it (a manager, store, observer or table instance) outlives that scope.",
  "defect_location": {
    "repo": "tldraw",
    "summary": "tldraw/packages/editor/src/lib/editor/managers/ScribbleManager/ScribbleManager.ts is the long-lived, editor-owned manager for all in-flight scribble trails, including the laser pointer's fading trail. Its item type carries no page: ScribbleItem is declared as {id, scribble, timeoutMs, delayRemaining, prev, next} at ScribbleManager.ts:7-14, and the internal Session it belongs to is {id, items, state, options, idleTimeoutHandle, fadeElapsed, totalPointsAtFadeStart} at ScribbleManager.ts:48-56 - neither records the page the trail was started on. The sessions are held for the editor's whole lifetime in `private sessions = new Map<string, Session>()` at ScribbleManager.ts:60, and tick/tickSession/tickSessionItems (ScribbleManager.ts:276-353) keep advancing and publishing every session each animation frame with no page predicate, so a laser trail started on page A keeps animating and drawing after the user switches to page B. The lookup methods compound it by searching across pages: addPoint iterates `for (const session of this.sessions.values())` at ScribbleManager.ts:281-296, and complete (:305-316) and stop (:324-334) do the same. Nothing resets the manager on page change - the only teardown entry points are clearSession (:187-195), complete(session) (:236-243) and reset (:264-270), all driven by the tool rather than by the active page. That the omission is a defect and not a design choice is settled by the folder's other managers: a verbatim bulk grep for 'PageId' over all 18 files in packages/editor/src/lib/editor/managers/ returns 10 matches in SpatialIndexManager/SpatialIndexManager.ts, 4 in CollaboratorsManager/CollaboratorsManager.ts and 1 in SnapManager/SnapManager.ts - the siblings that scope their state to a page - and zero matches in ScribbleManager.ts, ClickManager/ClickManager.ts, EdgeScrollManager/EdgeScrollManager.ts, InputsManager/InputsManager.ts, TickManager/TickManager.ts, FontManager/FontManager.ts and HistoryManager/HistoryManager.ts, each of which likewise owns transient interaction state on a page-independent lifetime. The contract is published outward through packages/editor/src/index.ts:118 (`type ScribbleItem`), confirmed as the sole re-exporter by cross_repo_lookup on symbol ScribbleManager, and the payload it publishes is the TLScribble record at packages/tlschema/src/misc/TLScribble.ts, consumed by packages/tldraw/src/lib/overlays/ScribbleOverlayUtil.ts and CollaboratorScribbleOverlayUtil.ts."
  },
  "case_id": "xrepo-v5-17-ephemeral-state-outlives-its-scope-medium",
  "case_question": "Transient visual feedback in one of these libraries outlives the thing it belongs to: the fading trail left behind by a presentation pointer keeps animating and drawing after the user has switched to a different page of the same document, because the component that owns those in-flight trails is one long-lived manager that never recorded which page each trail was started on, and nothing tears them down when the active page changes. The same shape of bug is a standing contract across the folder: short-lived state - the transient status of an in-flight fetch, a pending selection, an interaction still in progress - is owned by something that outlives the scope the state actually belongs to, and has to be reset when that scope changes underneath it. Which files own scope-bound ephemeral state of this kind and would have to change?",
